would_veto:
rea350: launch alongside an NGSA launch, on three grounds:
- balance sheet: about $6.67B a year in 2030-2035, above the $5.2B line;
- two fronts: two new launches in one round, with five years of development overlap against a two-year limit;
- downside: the item value is -2.15 in S6 and S7, beyond the $2B limit, even though it gains about +1.75 in S1-S5.
An override would also fail. This is a Qualified Majority item, and NGSA (Alt) + A350 Re-engine trails the best passing plan in the worst plausible column (S7: -1.34 against +0.81). Acceptable alternative: hold.

red_lines:
THE BOARD'S WOULD-VETO LIST:
- no Board item that loses more than $2B in any column S1-S7;
- one programme at a time;
- no equity raise or dividend cut;
- no override of Wagner's safety, quality, A320 ramp-shield or supply-crunch results;
- every red-line flag removed or rebutted in writing [AX-0008, AX-0026, A-0299].

tests.numbers:
rea350 is at hold. Earliest window on my capacity line: launch in 2035 (EIS 2040). It overlaps NGSA development 2035-2037 (2 years), with $1.2B of strain, which is at my limit. It also needs:
- no 787 Re-engine (rule 3);
- at least $1B on that round's grid;
- Toepfer's cash line, which on present bills fails any overlap year.
The clean window is after NGSA development rolls off in 2037.

Launch in 2030, the last window year, gives EIS 2037. Since the 2025 milestones (Wing of Tomorrow final load tests, the run-at-rate wing demonstrator) there have been five more years of maturation. That is an inference: the brief reports no wing or production-system status.
Slip prior: 1-2 years. A one-year slip gives 2038, outside 'around 2037' but inside 'the second half of the 2030s'.
Main slip risks:
- the wing at rate;
- new-engine readiness: UltraFan NB ready 2037, with no margin; GTF2 ready 2036;
- the production system matured with the aircraft;
- talent;
- our own work packages, titanium and rare earths.
Containment:
- CFM is always fitted, so no engine holds EIS;
- certify engines in sequence;
- the Alt plan cushions a slow NGSA ramp;
- KPI-gated rates;
- our own packages and materials secured first.

No inject this round. Readiness by NGSA EIS 2037, if each maker launches in 2030:
- CFM: always available;
- P&W GTF2 (launch_if_selected): ready 2036, one year of margin;
- the Joint Venture engine, if formed in 2030: ready 2036;
- RR UltraFan NB: ready 2037, no margin.
Rule 8 cannot bind: an unready maker is dropped and does not delay NGSA.

Combined capacity in 2038-2042, a month, on my reading of the step years (NGSA ramp interpolated to 50/80/100 at 4/6/8 years):
- Alt: about 94 / 87 / 89 / 92 / 79;
- base: about 84 / 77 / 79 / 64 / 65;
- today: 85.
Base leaves a trough of about 20 a month in 2041-2042 while demand climbs to 2,090 in 2042, and the rules hand our slack to Boeing.
Alt keeps mature A320 lines running: about 1,500 more aircraft for about $0.75B (my step reading gives about 1,370). The A320 never exceeds 82 a month, so LEAP-1A/GTF are never asked for more than today's rate. The NGSA ramp is unchanged at 50/80/100.
Alt item on the grid: +1.43 / +1.80 / +1.24 / +1.44 / +1.80 / +1.43 / +1.80.
Conditions:
- LEAP-1A and GTF supply for the tail to about 2045 contracted at launch;
- each A320 step-down gated on quality KPIs;
- NGSA staffing from recruitment, since Alt holds 10 / 10 / 10 / 28 a month more A320 rate in 2038-2041.
Pass on safety, quality, ramp shield and supply crunch.

Code 7:
- CFM always fitted;
- triggers both conditional launches: GTF2 ready 2036, UltraFan NB ready 2037;
- covers the Joint Venture engine (2036 if formed in 2030);
- 2050 sources and makers: 1/1 if the makers hold, 3/3 if both launch.
Codes 5 and 6 give two sources at best; code 3 gives one.
No forbidden (fps, NGSA) pair involves NGSA code 7.
Integration load: three engines mean more certification work than two. Certify in sequence, as on the A320neo (P&W November 2015, CFM May 2016). The code can be narrowed to 5 or 6 in round 2, before EIS, if the load threatens the 2037 date.

Nothing in the lean is covert.
Public lines that stand up:
- EIS as a window, 'the second half of the 2030s';
- the Alt plan as keeping A320 lines running through the NGSA ramp, a 'disciplined and controlled' ramp;
- recruitment and supply security limited to our own hiring, work packages and materials.
Avoid:
- the 2037 date;
- naming the engine supplier behind the 2025 reset;
- any word of Delay Tactics.

ops.would_change_mind_if:
A320 plan: I would revert to base in three cases:
- CFM or P&W cannot commit LEAP-1A/GTF supply for the Alt tail to about 2045;
- a brief reports a supply crunch, or a quality escape on the A320 lines or the NGSA production system, that makes combined output above today's 85 a month unsafe;
- the Alt item loses its grid margin.

A350 Re-engine: it comes back in round 2 (2035) only if all of these hold:
- the overlap is 2 years or less ($1.2B strain);
- the A350F and the Spirit packages are absorbed;
- Boeing has no 787 Re-engine;
- that round's grid shows at least $1B in every plausible column;
- Thomas's cash line holds.
The clean window is after NGSA development rolls off in 2037.

Engine code: I would narrow from 7 to 5 or 6, never dropping CFM, only if round 2 shows three-engine integration threatening the 2037 EIS or quality. If only one of P&W or Rolls-Royce launches, code 7 still gives CFM plus that maker, so no change.

NGSA: only a reported technology or engine-maturity slip would move it. I would then re-time it, never cancel it.

Delay Tactics: nothing this round. Later, only after a public fps launch, poaching only, and once.

memo_weighing.cfo:
Toepfer's tests are role-derived (Low confidence); I weighed them as the cash and risk case the Audit Committee will test.

Passed for the lean:
- peak annual cash, $5.07B against $5.2B with $0.13B of headroom;
- robustness, with both items positive in every column and the worst item +3.60 in S4;
- rule 6, one launch and no overlap;
- net cash, payout and buyback;
- Board items;
- the five pillars and internal control.

Failed, with binding vetoes I accept:
- option 5, on rule 6, $6.67B peak cash and -2.15 in S6-S7. This is a red line; no override is available;
- every Delay Tactics row, on rule 4: naked, with the $48.32B fine;
- option 4, a soft veto: robustness fails at -0.64 in S6-S7, and the option trails the lean everywhere, so no override.

None of these vetoes touches the chosen plan. I accept two conditions in full:
- the Alt-plan cash is phased from 2037, with no more than $0.13B in any year from 2030 to 2036;
- the earliest A350 development start his line allows is 2037.

I take his payout flag (net cash near zero around 2035 at a 48-50% payout on flat FCF) to the Board. Each year's payout is set inside the policy and calibrated to keep net cash non-negative. No equity raise.

Accepted as written. No override and no revision needed.

