recommendations.text:
Safety oversight: Mr Wagner should report to the Board's twice-yearly safety review on three things:
- NGSA production readiness: the wing at rate, the production system and engine maturity;
- the quality KPIs that gate each A320 step-down;
- the A320neo engine tail contracts.
Quality comes before rate at every step.

memo_weighing.cfo:
Weighted heavily, as the team rule and my profile require: I trust Neil's cash numbers over my operating dates.

His arithmetic matches mine and the brief's grid:
- launch_if_selected / hold scores -3.74 on my framing weights and -5.30 on his;
- the launch edge is -0.69, or -0.54 with the 2035 cancel;
- the envelope fit is 5.6%;
- the dividend is covered.

His two binding vetoes stand and I respect them in full. Ground 2 strikes the unconditional launch; ground 4 strikes a Joint Venture commit. Neither applies to the orders I submit.

I blended his column weights with my framing weights (S1 0.325, S6 0.175). His heavier weight on not-selected columns reflects GTF reputation risk in campaigns. I accept that as a real selection risk, not a reason to hedge.

I adopted his 2035 cancel rule as written. I also adopted his advice to publish no time-on-wing number until it is proven. He does not claim a payback year, and neither do I.

memo_weighing.ops:
Weighted on readiness, durability, parts and engineering load, where I defer to him. His seat rests on one event (June 2023, before the recall). He says so himself, so his durability plan and parts gate are seat doctrine and inference rather than his record. I discounted his dates accordingly, as he asked.

His readiness check binds and passes: 2036 against EIS 2037 and 2040. His parts check passes, though only because the bulletin shows nothing live and the board does not model parts.

His most useful contribution is the thin-margin warning: one year to a 2037 EIS, against a +1.5-2 year slip prior. It matches my own record of slipped operating dates. I adopted all his protections:
- an Advantage derivative;
- front-loaded severe-environment testing;
- full-life parts at EIS;
- the date gives, not the testing;
- the production ramp gated on fleet spares;
- a re-test in 2035.

I recorded his three conditions under which launch_if_selected should not stand: a live quality escape or rising AOG without containment; any compression of testing; and data showing durability cannot be proven by 2037. I took his statement addition.

He correctly declined to veto the unconditional launch on technical grounds. His fleet-first advice is consistent with the strike Mitchill's gate already required.

